title: Hold, or load
mode: vhdl
chapter: Memory
check: behaves
cycles: 100
random: load=0.3
---- intro
A flip-flop takes a new value at **every** clock edge. A memory that is useful keeps its value until you **ask** it to change. Here `load` says when: at a rising edge, if `load` is 1, `q` takes `d`; otherwise it keeps what it has.

That is an `if` inside the clock edge, with no `else`:

```
if rising_edge(clk) then
    if load = '1' then
        q <= d;
    end if;
end if;
```

When nothing gives `q` a new value, a flip-flop keeps the old one. This one is four bits wide: a **register**, as processors have.
---- task
Make the 4-bit `q` take `d` at a rising edge of `clk` when `load` is 1, and keep its value otherwise.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity reg4 is
  port(
    clk  : in  std_logic;
    load : in  std_logic;
    d    : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    q    : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end reg4;

architecture rtl of reg4 is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity reg4 is
  port(
    clk  : in  std_logic;
    load : in  std_logic;
    d    : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    q    : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end reg4;

architecture rtl of reg4 is
begin
    process (clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            if load = '1' then
                q <= d;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Start from the process of the flip-flop lesson, and put the `q <= d;` inside `if load = '1' then ... end if;`.
---- hint2
Inside `if rising_edge(clk) then`: `if load = '1' then q <= d; end if;`
---- solution
```
process (clk)
begin
    if rising_edge(clk) then
        if load = '1' then
            q <= d;
        end if;
    end if;
end process;
```
Change `d` with `load` off and press Step: `q` keeps its value.
---- done
A register holds a number until you tell it to change. A processor has a row of them, and an instruction says which one to load.
